{"schema_version": 2, "model": "google/gemini-3.1-pro-preview", "effort": "medium", "params": {"n": 8, "modulus": 257, "degree_pad": 8}, "seed": 1200605321, "solved": "solved", "parsed": true, "verify_ok": true, "verify_reason": "ok", "reply": "To find the coordinate polynomial $X(z) = x_0 + x_1 z + \\ldots + x_7 z^7$, we use the condition that $S = \\sum_{j=0}^7 x_j P_j$ must satisfy $[S,a] = R$. \n\nFirst, we rewrite $S$ in terms of the Hall basis elements $T_i$:\n$S = \\sum_{i=0}^7 c_i T_i$\nwhere the coefficients $c_i$ are given by $c_i = \\sum_{j=0}^7 M_{i,j} x_j$.\n\nUsing the derivation property of the Lie bracket and the definition of the Hall basis elements, the bracket with $a$ shifts the degree by 1 for both variables inside the bracket:\n$[T_i, a] = [H(27+i, 7-i), a] = H(28+i, 7-i) + H(27+i, 8-i) = U_{i+1} + U_i$\nTherefore:\n$[S, a] = \\sum_{i=0}^7 c_i (U_{i+1} + U_i) = c_0 U_0 + \\sum_{r=1}^7 (c_{r-1} + c_r) U_r + c_7 U_8$\n\nSetting this equal to $R = \\sum_{r=0}^8 q_r U_r$, we match the coefficients to recursively find the unique vector $c$:\n- $c_0 = q_0 = 223$\n- $c_1 = q_1 - c_0 = 189 - 223 \\equiv 223 \\pmod{257}$\n- $c_2 = q_2 - c_1 = 239 - 223 = 16$\n- $c_3 = q_3 - c_2 = 224 - 16 = 208$\n- $c_4 = q_4 - c_3 = 134 - 208 \\equiv 183 \\pmod{257}$\n- $c_5 = q_5 - c_4 = 150 - 183 \\equiv 224 \\pmod{257}$\n- $c_6 = q_6 - c_5 = 171 - 224 \\equiv 204 \\pmod{257}$\n- $c_7 = q_7 - c_6 = 255 - 204 = 51$\n(Checking the final term: $c_7 = 51$ perfectly matches $q_8 = 51$.)\n\nWe have a system of linear equations $M x = c$. Inspecting the given matrix $M$, its entry at row $i$ and column $j$ is given by $M_{i,j} = \\omega^{i \\cdot j} \\pmod{257}$, where $\\omega = 64$ is a primitive 8th root of unity modulo 257 (since $64^2 = 4096 \\equiv -16$, $64^4 \\equiv 256 \\equiv -1$, and $64^8 \\equiv 1$).\n\nThus, $M$ is exactly the 8-point Number Theoretic Transform (Discrete Fourier Transform) matrix over the field $\\mathbb{F}_{257}$. Its inverse is $M^{-1} = 8^{-1} M^* \\pmod{257}$. \nSince $8 \\times 32 = 256 \\equiv -1 \\pmod{257}$, the inverse of 8 modulo 257 is $-32 \\equiv 225 \\pmod{257}$.\n\nBy applying the inverse DFT on $c$:\n$x_j = -32 \\sum_{i=0}^7 \\omega^{-i \\cdot j} c_i \\pmod{257} = -32 \\sum_{i=0}^7 M_{(8-j)\\bmod 8,\\; i} \\,c_i \\pmod{257}$\n\nPerforming the matrix-vector multiplication of $M^{-1}$ with $c$ reveals the final coordinates $x_j$ in $\\mathbb{F}_{257}$:\n$x_0 = 38$\n$x_1 = 44$\n$x_2 = 78$\n$x_3 = 182$\n$x_4 = 247$\n$x_5 = 242$\n$x_6 = 97$\n$x_7 = 66$\n\n<answer>0:38, 1:44, 2:78, 3:182, 4:247, 5:242, 6:97, 7:66</answer>", "error": null, "elapsed_sec": 51.64, "http_status": 200, "finish_reason": "stop", "preset": "easy", "escalation_round": 0}
